TWO OF THE THREE PROBLEMS, IN ONE PATCH BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE SURFACE.

── THREE LAYERS, BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT A BOARD IS ──────────────────────────

It said five: finish, wear layer, fastening, moisture control, substrate.
Every one of those is real, but they are not one object. Three of them are
things that happen UNDERNEATH a board rather than parts of it, and drawing
them as a single exploded stack stated that a board has five layers. It
does not.

What is drawn now is an engineered board — real hardwood over a 90°
cross-ply core — and every definition is the one this site ALREADY
publishes in lib/glossary.ts, quoted rather than rewritten:

  wear layer      "The thickness of real hardwood above the core — what
                   determines how many times a floor can be refinished."
  cross-ply core  "each layer oriented at 90° to the one beside it"; "Each
                   layer's tendency to move across its own grain is resisted
                   by the layer bonded to it at a right angle."

A test now fails if the homepage and the glossary drift apart.

The reference for this was another manufacturer's page describing THEIR
board: a two-layer solid core with a UV-cured urethane finish. Ecowoods
supplies solid or engineered and does not sell that product, so none of
that wording is here — a test asserts it never arrives. The construction
shown is named on the page so nobody has to guess which one it is.

The three installation questions were the best part of the old version and
are NOT discarded: they become UNDER_THE_BOARD, rendered as a separate
short list under the heading "and three more the quote has to answer, which
are not part of the board", numbered 04–06. They belong to the quote, not
to the board.

The core is now drawn as a core: its grain runs ACROSS the boards above it,
because that opposition is the entire reason the layer exists and is the
only thing that makes it legible as a core rather than a second floor. The
first attempt ran the grain at 8° — almost parallel — and read as slate;
the one before that used 1px stripes, which moiré into a wire mesh at this
rake on a 2× screen.

── EIGHTEEN CHOICES, AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS NEW ───────────────────────────

Five species and nothing else became all four axes this business sells on:
5 species, 5 finishes, 4 patterns, 4 board widths. Every list is the
catalogue's own — FLOOR_PRODUCTS, FINISH_OPTIONS, PATTERN_OPTIONS,
BOARD_WIDTHS — not a copy, so the homepage cannot come to offer a finish
that /design does not have and pricing does not price.

ALL FOUR CHANGE THE PICTURE, from the catalogue's own data. The species
selects the photographed crop; the finish multiplies its published `tint`
into every board and drives the top coat from its published `sheen`, so a
natural matte returns visibly less light than a satin; the pattern lays the
boards; and the width sets how many there are — 3¼″ strip lays 126 boards
where 8″-and-wider lays 27, from the real inches rather than a hand-made
table. A choice that did not change the picture would be a decoration
pretending to be a configurator, and a test asserts the board count follows
the inches in the right direction for every width.

WIDTH IS DELIBERATELY NOT HANDED OFF. FloorConfigurator reads exactly four
parameters — species, finish, pattern, sqft — and has no board-width
control at all, so `width=` would be a parameter nobody reads: the visitor
picks 3¼″ strip, sees it, and arrives at a configurator with no trace of
it. Showing a width we cannot hand over is honest; pretending to hand it
over is the third instance of the silent drop this file already documents.
Finish DOES carry, and is validated against FINISH_OPTIONS the same way the
pattern is.

── IT HAD TO LOOK ALIVE, NOT MERELY BE MOVING ─────────────────────────────

The stack was already animating and still read as a still picture. 15° of
yaw over 26 seconds is real motion and is below the threshold at which a
person glancing at it believes it moves at all. It now turns 24°, over 19
seconds, through three keyframe stops rather than two so the turn does not
retrace its own path, and the three layers float 30px on 8/11/13s periods —
one reversed — against that 19s drift, so the assembly never returns to the
same pose inside a visit. Still only the stack and the layers animate; the
boards never do.

── VERIFICATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

413 assertions. Five mutations introduced and each one caught: width
hard-coded so it stops changing the picture, a width sent to a page that
cannot read it, the competitor's product claim copied into a layer name,
the motion amplitude reverted, and the finish reduced to a label.

parse-scan caught one of MY OWN test assertions: a literal ${...} inside a
plain string, which renders verbatim. It was intentional there and the
guard was still right to refuse it — the assertion now reads the single
`backgroundImage` declaration and checks the tint is in the board's own
background stack, which is both unambiguous and a stronger test.

65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css cannot run in this sandbox (postcss
unresolvable, npm 403) and fails identically on a clean checkout. The
stylesheet was parsed by Chromium instead and the section server-rendered
and screenshotted at every step. Typecheck clean.

