-
-Everything below was measured in a browser against this stylesheet, at 390px
-and at 1280px, not read off a screenshot.
-
-── THE MOBILE MENU WAS A TRAP ─────────────────────────────────────────────
-
-.topbar was `position: static` with `z-index: 80`. z-index does nothing on a
-static element, so the bar declared 80 and got none of it: .mobile-sheet is
-`position: fixed; z-index: 70`, and a POSITIONED element paints above a
-static one whatever the numbers say.
-
-Hit-testing the centre of the hamburger with the drawer open,
-`document.elementFromPoint` returned DIV.mobile-sheet. The close button
-rendered, was visible, and could not be tapped. Escape still worked — on a
-phone there is no Escape. The only way out of the menu was to navigate
-somewhere.
-
-`position: relative` with no offsets. It moves nothing, and it does not
-change the mega panel's containing block: .mm-panel is absolute against
-.topbar-inner, which is nearer and already relative. The drawer's own
-`padding-top: 6rem` has been reserving room for a header it was painting
-over.
-
-── TEN ROWS, TEN DIFFERENT RULES ──────────────────────────────────────────
-
-Row 07 of the drawer rendered at Plus Jakarta Sans 16px with no padding
-while every row around it was Fraunces 29.6px with 17.6px. Its "07" rendered
-at the row's own display face, three times the size of every other number in
-the list, because `.mobile-sheet nav a span.num` is scoped to `a` and row 07
-is a <button>.
-
-`.mnav-search` carried `font: inherit` and no size at all. Its own comment
-said it was "styled to sit in the same numbered list as its neighbours",
-which is the kind of sentence that stops anyone checking.
-
-Row typography now exists ONCE, for links, group triggers, the search row and
-the portal trigger together. A fourth row type joins the selector instead of
-copying six declarations. Measured after: all ten rows Fraunces 29.6px,
-17.6px padding, 12px mono number, one 1px rule between each.
-
-── THE FOOTER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
-
-Learn was 28 links in one flat column and Services 17. A list that long is
-not a menu, it is a scroll: nothing in it is findable because nothing in it
-is grouped.
-
-Eight named clusters — What we do / Get a price / Trade & referrals, and The
-company / Tools / Reference / Reading / Our work — using the same mono eyebrow
-the drawer's group titles use, so the two navigations read as one system. Not
-a second accordion: the column is already a <details> on mobile and nesting
-one inside it puts two taps between a person and a link.
-
-EVERY LINK IS PRESERVED. 45 unique hrefs before, 45 after, none lost and none
-invented.
-
-Then the height, measured at 1280px:
-
-    original                                  2,370px    —
-    split the column at its old width         1,504px   28/56 labels wrapped
-    no split, wider tracks                    1,783px    0 wrapped
-    SHIPPED: double the track, then split     1,289px    1/56 wrapped
-
-Splitting a 250px column into two 110px ones is not a saving, it is the same
-text in a worse shape. The track is widened first. The ratios were swept and
-measured rather than chosen; 0.9/1.3/2.3/0.9 was the only one that held at
-both 1280px and 1440px. The breakpoint is 1024px, not 1100, because the
-columns become accordions at max-width 1023px and any higher floor leaves a
-band where the footer is neither split nor collapsed.
-
-And the 44px tap target is kept where fingers are: `pointer: fine` asks about
-the INPUT rather than the screen, so a touch laptop at 1280px keeps the target
-a finger needs and only a mouse gets the compact rows.
-
-── THREE CSS MISTAKES I MADE WRITING THIS, RECORDED IN THE FILE ───────────
-
-Each was caught by measuring rather than by reading:
-
-  1. The `pointer: fine` override was written a thousand lines before the rule
-     it overrides. Same specificity, so `min-height: 44px` won and the only
-     thing that survived was the new padding — the row came out 48px, FOUR
-     PIXELS TALLER than the one it was written to shrink.
-  2. The grid override was written six hundred lines before the base
-     `.footer-grid`, and did nothing at all.
-  3. `.footer-group-label` is (0,1,0) and `.footer-grid p` is (0,1,1), so the
-     headings rendered at 16px body sans — larger than the links beneath them.
-     Moving it later did not help and could not: specificity, not order.
-
-── WHAT WAS CHECKED AND FOUND FINE ────────────────────────────────────────
-
-Reachability, because "no orphan pages" is a claim worth testing rather than
-asserting: 84 routes, 45 public and static, and 43 of them are reachable from
-the header, mega-menu or footer. The two that are not are /design/spec, which
-is where the configurator sends you after you configure something, and /r,
-the referral redirect. Neither is a nav destination. There is no orphan
-problem and this patch does not invent one.
-
-`useIsMobile` starts false, so the SERVER renders the expanded footer and the
-accordions appear after hydration. That is the right way round — every link is
-in the HTML for a crawler — and it is left alone.
-
-65 of 66 guards pass. verify:css cannot run in this sandbox (postcss
-unresolvable, npm 403) and fails identically on a clean checkout;
-verify:hygiene fails on ECOWOODS_CAM_01_edge_refinement.patch, which is an
-uploaded envelope already at the repo root and not part of this change.
-parse-scan clean.
